                U.S. Naval Observatory Nautical Almanac Office
                          Floppy Almanac User's Guide


1. Introduction

The Floppy Almanac is a program for MS-DOS based microcomputers which
reproduces the data contained in the major sections of the Astronomical, Air, 
and Nautical Almanacs to full precision.  The time span of each edition of the
Floppy Almanac is 400 days: one calendar year plus about two weeks overlap into
each of the adjacent years.

To properly use the Floppy Almanac, you must be familiar with basic
astronomical terminology, coordinate systems, and conventions.  You should be
familiar with the contents and use of either the Astronomical Almanac, the
Nautical Almanac, or the Air Almanac, depending on your particular application. 


To assist you with some of the specialized astronomical terminology, however, a
glossary is provided in Appendix B of this document.

All of the necessary files for the Floppy Almanac are contained on a single
5Ä1/4" DSDD disk.  The program is compatible with MSÄDOS 2.00 and higher and
runs on the IBM PC, XT, and AT computers and compatible machines.  There are 8
files in this version of the Floppy Almanac.  They are:

MESSIER.CAT    Messier catalog
RADIO1.CAT     extragalactic radio sources
STAR1.CAT      FK5 star catalog
FA.DFT         ASCII file of initial values
CATALOG.EXE    catalog generation utility
FAyy.EXE       the main program
READ.ME        more information
SAMPLE.TXT     sample input for generating catalogs

The first file in the list is the executable module comprising the Floppy
Almanac, and is the only essential file.  Its file name is formed by replacing
the characters yy with the last two digits of its year, for example, FA88.EXE
for the 1988 edition of the Floppy Almanac.  It is executed by simply typing
the first four characters, that is, "fa88" (or"FA88") at the DOS prompt for the
1988 Floppy Almanac or the corresponding codes for other years.  The module
will then be read from the disk into the computer's memory and the Floppy
Almanac masthead and main menu will appear on the screen.  Once the main menu
appears the disk can be removed from the drive; no further disk reads are
required for standard execution.

The other files on the disk are auxiliary files, which are not used by most
applications.  Most of these files relate to the optional facility for
redefining the star catalog to be used by the Floppy Almanac and are described
in detail below.

Version 2.00.xx of the Floppy Almanac is written in FORTRAN and assembly
language and has been compiled to take advantage of an 8087 or 80287 numeric


   1988 US Naval Observatory Nautical Floppy Almanac Version 2.00.88  Page  1

coprocessor, if one is installed in your computer.  A coprocessor is not
required; however, the Floppy Almanac will run up to 5 times faster with a
coprocessor.  The program requires about 180k bytes of RAM, therefore a machine
with at least 256k bytes of RAM will be required, depending on the version of
MSÄDOS in use.  The program does not use any screen graphics -- all output is
in text mode and the monitor and keyboard are used as a "dumb terminal".


2. Getting Started -- Sample Run

Since the Floppy Almanac is menu and prompt driven, the novice user can usually
"feel" his way around its various options and obtain useful information.  One
can become fairly proficient in using the Floppy Almanac simply through a
series of trial-and-error runs.  Nevertheless, there is a significant amount of
information about how the Floppy Almanac works which is not obvious but which
is quite important for all but the most casual user.  This information is
presented below.

The following is a simple, but typical, run of the Floppy Almanac which
illustrates its use.  This run produces the rise, set, and transit times of
Mars for five consecutive dates.  You may wish to duplicate this run to get
acquainted with the Floppy Almanac.  Data which you must type appears below
underlined.  Menu selections are made using the 'F - keys' on the computer
keyboard.

This run is from version 2.00.88.  This run is for 1988; if you have a Floppy
Almanac for a different year replace each occurrence of "88" below with the
last two digits of the appropriate year.  Obviously for years other than 1988
the output data values will be different than those shown but the input and
output formats will be the same.

Start the Floppy Almanac by typing "fa88".

