3. Supported Celestial Objects

One of the features of the Floppy Almanac is that the most common tabulations
are supported for a wide range of celestial objects.  For example, "F6 Rise &
Set Times" can be used not only for the Sun and Moon, but also for planets or
stars.  The example above shows a tabulation for Mars, but the object could
have been Vega, the Moon, Pluto, Canopus, Eta Tauri, etc.

For the solar system, the Floppy Almanac contains information on the positions
of all nine major planets plus the Sun and Moon.  The information is internally
stored as a Chebyshev series for each of three position vector components (with
respect to the solar system barycenter) for each of the eleven solar system
bodies.  The series "work" only within the 400-day window of time which defines


   1988 US Naval Observatory Nautical Floppy Almanac Version 2.00.88  Page  5
the span of validity for the Floppy Almanac disk.  If, during a tabulation, a
date outside this span is encountered, the error message
'Julian date before first allowed date.' or'Julian date after last allowed
date.
is displayed and the tabulation is terminated.

The basic catalog of stars consists of data for the brightest 199 stars in the
FK5 catalog, plus one navigational star (Sabik: Eta Ophiuchi) not in the FK5. 
Stars as faint as third magnitude are represented, although the catalog is not
complete at this level.  The catalog is stored within the Floppy Almanac module
and consists of the names, epoch J2000.0 position and proper motion, parallax
and other information for each star.

This basic 200 star catalog can be replaced.  One of the main menu choices, "F9
Change Catalog", can be used to specify an external disk file to be used in
place of the basic star catalog.  Three catalog files are provided on the
distribution disk:  the entire FK5 catalog, a catalog of compact extragalactic
radio sources, and the Messier catalog of clusters and nebulae.  You can also
set up your own catalog of objects.  This will be discussed more thoroughly in
section 6.4 and Appendix A.


4. Specifying What You Want

During its execution, the Floppy Almanac requests you to enter two broad types
of input data: names and numbers.  The Floppy Almanac treats these two types of
input data quite differently and therefore requests them separately.  Names are
simply character strings used to select celestial objects or menu choices.  
Numbers are used to specify exactly what data you want the Floppy Almanac to
produce on any given run.  Numbers are used to supply information on dates and
intervals, geographic position (latitude and longitude), and the number of
lines of output to be produced.  The meaning, format, and general requirements
for all these types of input data are given below.


4.1  Names

In the context of the Floppy Almanac, a "name" is simply a string of one to
eight alphanumeric characters.  The Floppy Almanac always requests names
separately from numbers.  Lower case letters which you enter as part of a name
are converted to upper case by the Floppy Almanac, any blanks are treated as
meaningless spacers (ignored except for the fact that they take up one of the
eight positions), and any characters beyond the eighth position are
superfluous.  Therefore, "Alp Cen", "ALP CEN", and "a L pCENTAURI" are
completely equivalent names, since the Floppy Almanac will (internally) reduce
each to the basic form "ALPCEN ".  (Note:  When entering names, do not surround
them with quotes.)

Names are used to select celestial objects.  The following rule holds
throughout:

When entering names, you need type the fewest number of characters necessary to
unambiguously specify your choice.


   1988 US Naval Observatory Nautical Floppy Almanac Version 2.00.88  Page  6
Thus, for example, when you are asked to enter an object name there is never
any reason to type more than eight characters since only the first eight are
used.  Therefore, "ALPCENTAURI" is equivalent to "ALPCEN".  Furthermore,
according to the above rule, you need only enter the number of characters
necessary to distinguish your choice from any of the other possibilities.  It
may not be obvious how many characters are needed to unambiguously specify your
choice. For example, "ma" is sufficient to distinguish Mars from Mercury or the
Moon, but how about the star Markab?  Does "ve" result in Venus or Vega?  In
these cases it is helpful to know that the Floppy Almanac has an ordered list
of object names and will select the first object from the list that seems to
correspond to the name you entered.  The ordered list is as follows:

Sun,Moon,Mercury,Venus,Earth,Mars,Jupiter,Saturn,Uranus,Neptune,Pluto,Star
names:    Proper names,  Bayer designations, and DM numbers.

The stars in the 200-star catalog are arranged in the following order:(a)  the
57 standard navigational stars, in order of  navigational star number; followed
by(b)     the next 143 brightest FK5 stars, in order of     decreasing
brightness (increasing magnitude).

Thus, "s" and "m" are sufficient for the Sun and Moon, respectively, "ma" will
indeed get you Mars, not Markab ("Mark" is sufficient for the latter), "ve"
yields Venus, but "veg" is needed for Vega.

There are two "wild-card" characters which you can use within names.  The first
is "?" (question mark), which means "any single character is acceptable here". 
Thus, for example, if you were unsure of whether the star name "Alkaid" was
spelled with a "k" or a "c" in the middle, you could enter the name "Al?aid".  
The second "wild-card" character is "_" (underscore), which means "one or more
blanks must be present here".  This character is useful when blanks are
significant parts of names (normally blanks are ignored).  Examples are,
"Kaus_Aus", "+6_919", and "ALP_TAU", although in most cases omitting this
character will not result in any ambiguity.  Also, it turns out that because of
the order of the navigational stars, the names "den" or "deneb" will always
result in the selection of the star Denebola; to obtain Deneb, you must enter
"deneb_".

Note that star names may be specified as proper name, Bayer designation, or DM
number.  Each star in the 200-star catalog also has a serial number which can
be used as a name if it is known; serial numbers 1 to 57 are the standard
navigational star numbers.

Proper names have been taken from the list in the back of the Nautical Almanac,
supplemented by common names for some of the fainter stars taken from Appendix
II of the Yale Bright Star Catalog (4th ed).  However, note that there is no
universal agreement on the English spelling of many star names.

Bayer designations should be entered in the form "GGG CCC" where "GGG"
represents the first three letters of the English name of the Greek letter
("ALP", "BET", "GAM", etc.) and "CCC" represents the standard 3-letter IAU
constellation abbreviation ("ORI", "CAS", "UMA", etc.).  Thus "LAM SCO", "Alp
Lyr", and "gam cru" are acceptable Bayer designations.  Since some of the stars
are components of binary systems, it may be necessary to insert a component
number into the middle of the name, for example, "ALP2 LIB"; if you are unsure
about whether a component number is required, try "ALP?LIB" or the like.


   1988 US Naval Observatory Nautical Floppy Almanac Version 2.00.88  Page  7
DM numbers should not contain leading zeros in either the zone or the star
number subfields.  When the star number subfield requires four or fewer digits,
a "_" character is recommended to separate the two subfields.  The zone section
must always begin with a "+" or "-".  Thus "+3_419" is an acceptable DM number,
but not the apparently equivalent "03 0419".
Thus, any of the names "RIGEL", "11", "rig", "bet or", or "-8_1063" could be
used to specify the star Rigel.


4.2  Numbers

All numeric data that the Floppy Almanac requires to begin a calculation is
entered from the menu selection "F1 Initial Values".  Numeric data are entered
as FORTRAN "list-directed input" which is very free-form.  Floating point
numbers may be truncated at any point to the right of the decimal point, i.e.
the number is assumed to be extended on the right with zeros.  The decimal
point itself may be omitted if the fraction is zero.  A disadvantage of this
form of input is its insistence on only allowing number to be entered.  If a
letter or special character is entered when a number is expected, the error
message "Invalid character in input!  Try again." will appear.  Also, once a
numeric input has been chosen, a number must be entered.  If the enter key is
pressed without having first entered a number, the prompt is redisplayed.
Specific information on the three general types of numeric data follows.

4.2.1  Dates and Intervals

The date which you enter in response to a prompt from the Floppy Almanac is
used to specify the epoch for the tabulation.  For fixedÄinterval tabulations
this date is used as the beginning date of a running table; you must also
select the tabulation interval and the number of lines of output you wish.  The
tabulation interval is the difference in tabular epochs between successive
output lines.

As in the Astronomical Almanac, the independent argument for most Floppy
Almanac tabulations is dynamical time, specifically, Terrestrial Dynamical Time
(TDT).  TDT is effectively equal to TAI (International Atomic Time) + 32.184
seconds, and is the extension of the old Ephemeris Time scale, abandoned in
1984.  The more familiar Universal Time (UT), which is tied to the (irregular)
rotation of the Earth, is currently "slow" with respect to TDT and the TDT - UT
difference is now almost one minute.  A complete table of past and projected
values of TDT-UT, accurate to a fraction of a second, can be found on page K9
of the Astronomical Almanac.

Therefore, in using the Floppy Almanac, dates and intervals to be entered
should be expressed in Terrestrial Dynamical Time (TDT), except for the
following cases.  "F7 Navigation" and "F5 Sidereal Time" require Universal Time
(UT, or, more precisely, UT1), otherwise known as Greenwich Mean Time (GMT). 
Within the "F3 Positions" selection, the time scale for heliocentric and
barycentric coordinates is, technically, Barycentric Dynamical Time (TDB),
although TDB never differs from TDT by more than 1.7 milliseconds.

Dates may be entered in either of two forms:  
year-month-day-hour-minute-second, or Julian date and fraction.  The former is


   1988 US Naval Observatory Nautical Floppy Almanac Version 2.00.88  Page  8
entered using the format YYMMDD.HHMMSS, for example, 880314.052741 for 1988
March 14, 5:27:41 TDT.  Alternatively, a Julian date, including an appropriate
fraction, may be entered, for example, 2447234.72756 which corresponds to the
above date.  The Floppy Almanac can distinguish between the two forms since
they require, respectively, six and seven digits to the left of the decimal
point.

Intervals may also be entered in either of two formats:  
days-hours-minutes-seconds, or days and fraction.  The format of the interval
must correspond to the format of the date.  If the date is entered as
year-month-day-hour-minute-second, the interval must be entered as
days-hours-minutes-seconds, using the format DD.HHMMSS.  Similarly, if the date
is entered as Julian date and fraction, the interval must be entered in the
form days and fraction.  For example, if a tabulation interval of 8.5 minutes
is desired, it must be entered as 0.000830 if the date is entered as
880314.052741 and as 0.005903 if the date is entered as 2447234.72756.


4.2.2  Geographic Position - Latitude and Longitude

Latitude and longitude are always entered as degrees plus fraction.  North
latitudes are positive, south latitudes negative.  In accordance with a recent
IAU resolution, east longitudes are considered positive, west longitudes
negative.  For most purposes not more than two or three digits to the right of

the decimal point are required for latitude and longitude entries, since 0.01
degree of latitude (or longitude at the equator) corresponds to just over 1 km. 



Therefore the latitude and longitude of (downtown) Chicago could be expressed
as -87.63, 41.85; that of Sydney, Australia, 151.22, -33.88.


4.2.3 Number of Lines

The data item "F5 Number of Lines" is used to specify how many lines of output
you wish for the fixed interval tabulations.  The first line will show data for
the starting date which you entered, and each succeeding line will show the
data for one tabulation interval further in the future.


4.2.4 Time Zone

The menu selection "F6 Rise & Set Times" is the only section of the Floppy
Almanac which uses information about time zones.  Time zones are entered from
the "F1 Initial Values" menu selection.  The time zone value is the number of
hours before or after UT that is in effect for the longitude of the tabulation. 


Note however, that it is not necessary to enter the time zone value for the
longitude for which the phenomena is being calculated.  A value of 0 will
result in a tabulation in UT, while a value of -4 for longitude -77.00 will
produce a table in Eastern Daylight Time.


   1988 US Naval Observatory Nautical Floppy Almanac Version 2.00.88  Page  9
4.3 Default Values of Input Data

The Floppy Almanac will search 3 sources of initial values when starting a
calculation.  The first source is the values entered by the user from the "F1
Initial Values" menu selection.  These values may only be changed from the menu
selection.  The second source is the external disk file of values called
FA.DFT.  This is simply a file of ASCII text which is read by the Floppy
Almanac once at start-up time.  If this file is not found in the current
directory or is not readable for some reason, then the third source of initial
values will be used, which are values stored internal in the Floppy Almanac and
are not modifiable.  The FA.DFT file can be customized for individual uses and
can be edited by any ASCII text editor, and has the simple format that the
numbers must be in columns 21 thru 40.  The order of the lines is critical and
must not be changed, i.e. the first line must be the starting date, the second
line must be the latitude, etc.  Copying the supplied FA.DFT and renaming the
original will provide a pattern to use when setting up your own default values. 



The Floppy Almanac program only reads this file; it will not alter the contents
of the FA.DFT file.  Below is the contents of a typical FA.DFT file.
Starting date =     880601.00000Latitude     =    39.000Longitude = -77.000  
Time step = 1.00  Num. of positions =5   Time zone =-4


4.4 Choices at End of Tabulation

After the Floppy Almanac has computed and tabulated the data you requested, it
displays the statement:

Pause - Please enter a blank line (to continue) or a DOS command.
At this point, any DOS command may be executed, or the word "command" may be
typed to load a DOS shell.  While running the shell, keep in mind that
available RAM has been decreased by the amount occupied by DOS and the Floppy
Almanac (about 280k with DOS 3.2).  The command "exit" is used to leave the DOS
shell and return to the Floppy Almanac.  The cursor is turned off by the Floppy
Almanac and will not be visible in the DOS shell unless the DOS "mode" command
is used to reset the video mode.  See the DOS manual for instructions on this
command.  If the cursor is turned on in the shell, it will remain visible after
returning to the Floppy Almanac.  The key sequence "Alt-F1" will redraw the
header and menu and turn the cursor off.


5. File Output

The Floppy Almanac receives input from the keyboard and displays its output on
the monitor screen.  However, a new feature has been added with release
2.00.88.  Menu option "F2 File Output" will capture all of the titles, column
headers and numbers from a calculation to a disk file.  When "F2 File Output"
is selected, there are 3 situations which can occur:

#1 The named file does not exist.#2 The named file exists but is not opened for
writing.#3 The named file exists and is currently open.


   1988 US Naval Observatory Nautical Floppy Almanac Version 2.00.88  Page  10
The Floppy Almanac tests for these conditions and has a slightly different
response to each of them.  If the file does not exist, it is created and opened
for output.  If the file already exists, but is not open, it is opened and the
new data will be appended to the bottom of the file.  Finally, if an output
file exists and is open, it will be closed.  It can be re-opened later by
selecting "F2 File Output" and specifying the same file name.


6. Details of Floppy Almanac Computations and Output

As noted above, when the Floppy Almanac program is started, the main menu
appears.  It is always possible to return to the main menu after any tabulation
is finished by pressing the "Enter" key.  The main menu is used to select the
type of tabulation desired or otherwise control Floppy Almanac execution.  As
shown in the example in section 2, this menu presents 10 options.

A brief explanation of each of the options follows.  Except where specifically
noted, the data values produced by the Floppy Almanac will be consistent with
those printed in the Astronomical, Nautical, or Air Almanacs, with occasional
end-figure discrepancies of one unit.


6.1 Positions


If you select this option, a secondary menu immediately appears:

