Each of the options produces a tabulation of the positions of a specific object
with respect to one of four standard astronomical coordinate systems, as
described below.

"F1 Apparent geocentric coordinates" and "F2 Astrometric geocentric
coordinates" both produce tabulations of right ascension and declination, and,
for solar system objects, true distance.  Apparent coordinates are with respect


   1988 US Naval Observatory Nautical Floppy Almanac Version 2.00.88  Page  11
to the true equator and equinox of date; astrometric coordinates are with
respect to the mean equator and equinox of J2000.0.

"F3 Heliocentric coordinates" are tabulated as ecliptic longitude and latitude
and radius vector, with respect to the mean ecliptic and equinox of date. This
tabulation is provided only for solar system objects.

"F4 Barycentric rectangular coordinates" are tabulated as equatorial X, Y, and
Z, measured from the solar system barycenter.  The XY-plane is the mean equator
of J2000.0, the Z-axis points toward the mean north celestial pole of J2000.0,
and the X-axis points toward the mean equinox of J2000.0.  This tabulation is
provided only for solar system objects.


6.2 Physical Ephemeris of Planet

The physical ephemeris of a planet refers to its aspect as seen from the Earth:
its apparent magnitude, the angular size of its disk, its apparent degree of
illumination, the orientation of its pole, and the positions of its sub-solar
and sub-Earth points.  In the Astronomical Almanac, the physical ephemeris of a
planet is spread across two facing pages.  This divides into what could be
called "illumination data" on the left-hand pages and "rotation data" on the
right-hand pages.  The Floppy Almanac asks which set of data you wish to see. 
The data tabulated by the Floppy Almanac for the planets are the same as those
in the Astronomical Almanac.

The physical ephemeris of the Sun is not available from the Floppy Almanac. The
physical ephemeris of the Moon is available but is substantially different from
that in the Astronomical Almanac, both in format and accuracy.  The model of
the rotation of the Moon used by the Floppy Almanac is based on a simplified
lunar libration theory and therefore the rotation data are not as accurate as
that in the Astronomical Almanac.


6.3 Sidereal Times

This selection provides computed Greenwich mean and apparent sidereal times,
and local apparent sidereal time for any longitude.  This computation does not
depend on the Floppy Almanac's solar system ephemeris; therefore, it will work
for dates in any year.

6.4 Change Catalog

This menu selection is used to replace the standard 200-star catalog with
another catalog of objects.  The new catalog must exist as a
specially-formatted disk file external to the Floppy Almanac module.  A group
of such catalogs is provided on the Floppy Almanac disk:

Catalog    File      Contents                                 Number of
number     name                                               objects
1       Standard     200-star catalog                          200
2       STAR1.CAT    FK5 star data transformed to J2000.0     1536
3       RADIO1.CAT   Compact extragalactic radio sources       233
4       MESSIER.CAT  Messier catalog of clusters and nebulae   109


   1988 US Naval Observatory Nautical Floppy Almanac Version 2.00.88  Page  12
You will be prompted to "Enter catalog number or file name of catalog".  You
may enter one of the catalog numbers given above or the corresponding file
name.  The new catalog will be used for the duration of the Floppy Almanac run
(the standard 200-star catalog is always in place at the beginning of a run) or
until you again select "F9 Change Catalog".  If at any point you wish to
restore the standard 200-star catalog, simply select "F9 Change Catalog" and
respond to the prompt with a "1".  (The standard 200-star catalog does not have
a file name, since it is actually just the first 200 stars of catalog 2.)

You can create your own catalogs of special objects.  A program to set up your
catalog in the required format is supplied on the Floppy Almanac disk as file
CATALOG.EXE.  See Appendix A for further information.

The Floppy Almanac can internally store the data for 200 objects at a time.  As
part of the "F9 Change Catalog" routine, data on the first 200 objects in the
new catalog will be read into memory.  If you have selected a catalog with more
than 200 objects in it, the Floppy Almanac will execute disk reads whenever it
needs to access data for an object not currently in memory.

Once a new catalog has been defined, most Floppy Almanac tabulations (those
normally applicable to stars) can be requested for any of the new set of
objects.  Each object in each catalog has up to three names (aliases) by which
it can be referred:

Catalog FileName   How specified  Examples  number     name priority

1 & 2   STAR1.CAT  Name 1 Proper name ARCTURUS, MIZAR  Name 2 Bayer
designation    BET ORI, ALP2 CVN        Name 3    DM number +23 541, - 6 1241
3       RADIO1.CAT  Name 1    IAU designation     0851+202, 1641+399       
Name 2    3C or 4C number     3C 454, 4C-02.81         Name 3    Other
designation     OJ287, NRAO 150
4       MESSIER.CAT Name 1    Messier number M3, M57, M 31       Name 2    NGC
number    NGC 6205, NGC221         Name 3     Common name   RING, ANDROMED

The rules for referring to objects by name are given in section 4.1.  An
object's serial number within its catalog is also a "name" by which it can be
referred.

Redefinition of the catalog to be used by the Floppy Almanac does not affect in
any way the availability or accuracy of data for solar system objects.
Before the "F9 Change Catalog" routine exits, you will be asked whether you
wish to see a display of data for all the objects in the new catalog.  This
display lists the serial number, all three names, and the J2000.0 right
ascension and declination of each object.  The CATALOG.EXE utility program can
also produce a complete list of the contents of each catalog file, which may be
output as a disk file.

A few notes on each catalog:  Catalog 2 is the FK5, with the addition of one
star (Eta Ophiuchi) which is a standard navigational star not in the FK5.  The
FK5 contains stars as faint as visual magnitude 8, but is not complete except
for the very brightest stars.  The data in the other catalogs have been
transformed to equator, equinox, and epoch J2000.0 by Dr. Clayton Smith of the
Naval Observatory; the equinox is that of the FK5.  Catalog 3 is the list of


   1988 US Naval Observatory Nautical Floppy Almanac Version 2.00.88  Page  13
233 compact extragalactic radio sources compiled by a working group of IAU
Commission 24, and is the same as the list of radio sources published in the
1988 Astronomical Almanac.  Catalog 4 is the standard Messier catalog; the
serial numbers are the same as the "M" numbers.  M40, a "missing" Messier
object, has null data in its record.


6.5 Rise, Set, and Transit Times

This selection produces, for any object, a tabulation of ephemeris transit
times, and local times of rise, set, and transit for any location.
Additionally, the local azimuth at rise and set (measured eastward from true
north) and the local altitude at transit is displayed.  The times of rise and
set correspond to the times when the upper limb of the object appears to be
coincident with the true (unobstructed) horizon, with atmospheric refraction at
optical wavelengths and standard atmospheric conditions at sea level taken into
account.  Similarly, the computed altitude at transit includes the effects of
standard refraction.

The time zone used by the Floppy Almanac for the local rise, set and transit
times is the one specified in the "F1 Initial Values" menu choice.  Any time
zone may be specified for any longitude.  This makes a calculation possible for
UT, for example, or more convenient for Daylight Saving Time.  The time zone
used by the Floppy Almanac is displayed at the top of the tabulation:  for
example, "UT -6" is North American Central Standard Time, and "UT +1" is
central European time.

The following symbols may appear within the tabulation:
     ** **     means object is continually above horizon    -- --     means
object is continually below horizon     p    means event occurs on preceding
date, local time    f    means event occurs on following date, local time  s
means altitude is measured from southern horizon  n    means altitude is
measured from northern horizon

The rise and set times displayed by the Floppy Almanac for the Sun and Moon may
differ at high latitudes from those printed in the Astronomical or Nautical
Almanac by several minutes, especially near the times when the Sun or Moon is
continually above the horizon.  The rise and set times displayed by the Floppy
Almanac for latitudes very close to the poles are not, in general, reliable.


6.6 Navigation

This selection tabulates all the astronomical information necessary to obtain a
line of position from an altitude observation made at sea level.  The Floppy
Almanac will prompt you to enter the name of the object observed, the exact
time of the sight (in UT1 or GMT), and the assumed position (latitude and
longitude) from which the sight was made.  Once these are entered, the Floppy
Almanac will display both almanac data and altitude corrections for the object
specified.  The almanac data consist of:  Greenwich hour angle (GHA),
declination (Dec), computed altitude (Hc), and computed azimuth (Zn).  The
altitude corrections consist of atmospheric refraction (Refr) for sea level and
standard conditions, semidiameter (SD), parallax in altitude (PA), and the sum


   1988 US Naval Observatory Nautical Floppy Almanac Version 2.00.88  Page  14
Refr + SD + PA.  If the Sun is a factor at the time of the observation (daytime
or during nautical twilight), a similar line of data will also be displayed for
it.  Additionally, the GHA of Aries is always displayed.

If, in place of the name of a specific object, you enter "all" (or "ALL"), a
line of data will be displayed for each navigational planet and star which is
above the horizon as viewed from the assumed position at the time specified.
The altitude corrections are intended for use during sight reduction.  To
obtain the observed altitude (Ho), the sum of the altitude corrections (in the
rightmost column) may be added to the apparent altitude (ha), which itself is
obtained from the sextant altitude (hs) by removing instrumental and dip
corrections.  The altitude corrections strictly apply only in the case where
the observation was in fact made from the assumed position and, for solar
system objects, the lower limb of the specified object was observed. Generally,
however, these corrections are weak functions of altitude and can therefore be
applied, with some small error, to sights made close to the assumed position.  
The first of the listed corrections, refraction, applies to sea level
observations made under standard atmospheric conditions.  The semidiameter
correction for the Moon includes augmentation.

The tabulated quantities can also be used for observation planning, where a
prediction of the apparent altitude (ha) may be formed by subtracting the sum
of the altitude corrections from the computed altitude:  ha (predicted) = Hc -
Sum.  In many cases, Sum is itself negative, leading to ha (predicted) being
greater than Hc.

The tabulated almanac data are consistent with those printed in the Air or

Nautical Almanacs, to within the accuracy of the latter, 0.1-0.2 minutes of
arc.  The only significant difference (which may reach 0.5 minutes of arc) may
arise in the case of Venus, where, in the Floppy Almanac, no adjustment has
been made for its phase.  (If the disk of a planet can be resolved, then an
observation of its lower limb can be reduced in the same way as that for the
Sun or Moon; a phase correction is then irrelevant.) The Floppy Almanac's
altitude corrections are consistent, within 0.2 minutes of arc, with those
tabulated on the front inside cover of the Nautical Almanac.


6.7 Daily Configuration

This selection provides a concise summary of the most frequently used
information on the observability of all the major solar system bodies (Sun and
Moon and planets except Pluto), including right ascension and declination,
elongation from Sun, magnitude, apparent angular size of disk, etc.  The
information is arranged in order of elongation from Sun.  The only input
required is the date.


6.8  Topocentric Altitude and Azimuth

The altitude and azimuth of a celestial body as observed from any geographic
location at any time can be obtained by using the "F7 Navigation" selection. 
The celestial objects which can be specified are not limited to those normally


   1988 US Naval Observatory Nautical Floppy Almanac Version 2.00.88  Page  15
used for celestial navigation.  In fact, if you redefine the catalog in use
(see section 6.4), it is possible to obtain "F7 Navigation" on objects such as
the Ring Nebula or the quasar 3C 273.
For those not familiar with navigational notation, the following definitions
are provided:

GHA  Greenwich hour angle.  Apparent instantaneous hour angle of object
measured westward from the plane of the Greenwich meridian.

Dec  Declination.  Apparent declination of object.

Hc   Computed altitude.  Instantaneous altitude of center of object, calculated
using its geocentric position, that is, without adjustments for topocentric
parallax or refraction.

Zn   Computed azimuth.  Instantaneous azimuth of object, measured eastward from
true north.

Refr Refraction. Atmospheric refraction correction applicable to object, if
observed at sea level at optical wavelengths, under standard atmospheric
conditions.

SD   Semidiameter.  Half the apparent equatorial angular diameter of object.

PA   Parallax in altitude.  Topocentric parallax correction applicable to
object.

Sum  Sum = Refr + SD + PA.  If Sum is subtracted from Hc, the apparent altitude
(that comparable to observation) of the lower limb of the object is obtained.

7. Future Developments

This is the second major version of the Floppy Almanac to be released.  Many of
the changes that have been incorporated into its operation are the direct
result of letters received from satisfied (or irate) customers.  The most
popular request by far was to have some way of installing your local
coordinates as the default values.  The change made to implement this was the
FA.DFT file.  Other minor changes have been made, but at this point, they are
all in the appearance of the program and not in its operation.  The next
improvement will be the addition of civil, nautical, and astronomical twilight
times to the Rise & Set Times menu choice.  Improvements and extensions will be
made to the software, bugs will be fixed, inconvenient features changed, and
versions for computers of architectures other than that of the IBM PC may be
produced.  Versions of the Floppy Almanac which span more than one year are
still under study.  As the computing speed, memory, and disk capacity of
microcomputers increases, the Floppy Almanac will be changed to take advantage
of the increased computing power available.

Comments, criticisms, and suggestions are solicited.  Please mail your remarks
to:

Nautical Almanac Office Code FA U. S. Naval Observatory Washington, DC
20392-5100


   1988 US Naval Observatory Nautical Floppy Almanac Version 2.00.88  Page  16
Appendix A

Creating Star or Special-Object Catalogs

In order for catalog positions of stars or other special objects (e.g.,
pulsars, globular clusters, X-ray sources, etc.) to be used by the Floppy
Almanac, they must be in a specially-coded binary form.  Once in this form, the
catalog can be substituted for the standard 200-star catalog by using the "F9
Change Catalog" option, in which you specify the new catalog by entering its
MS-DOS file name.

A program is provided on the disk as file CATALOG.EXE which can be used to
transform a catalog of yours to the special binary form required by the Floppy
Almanac.  You can set up your catalog using an editor, another program, etc.
Your catalog must be formatted, that is, composed of character data, with one
record of 96 characters for each object in the catalog.  The order of the
records is not important, but each record in your catalog must be set up as
follows:

Field     Field     Contents  Units               Example  posn. format
1- 8      A8        Name1     Left justified      ALP2 LIB 9-16  A8
Name2, left justified    ---  ZUBENELG17-24  A8   Name3, left justified    ---
     -15 399625-38   F14.10    J2000.0 right ascension hours     
14.847975694639-52   F14.10    J2000.0 declination     degrees   
-16.041802501053-62 F10.4      J2000.0 proper motion in RA  s / cy    
-0.735063-72   F10.4     J2000.0 proper motion in dec  " / cy
-6.831073-80    F8.4     Parallax  "    0.049081-88    F8.4  Radial Velocity km
/ s   -10.000089-96 F8.4 Visual Magnitude (or flux)     mag, Jy   2.9000

The example column shows data for the star Zubenelgenubi, Alpha2 Librae.  Names
are truncated on the right, if necessary.  Any of the name fields may be left
blank.  The time unit for proper motion is the Julian century.  For stars, the
parallax and radial velocity fields can be set to zero if these quantities are
unknown.  For extragalactic objects, or those without precise positions, the
proper motion, parallax, and radial velocity fields can be set to zero.  The
visual magnitude (flux) field is not currently used by the Floppy Almanac.
A small sample catalog file formatted as above is included on the disk as file
SAMPLE.TXT .  It contains data on five stars.

To use the program to convert your catalog to the binary form suitable for use
by the Floppy Almanac, simply type CATALOG .  You will be prompted to enter the
file name of the catalog you are supplying as input (constructed as specified
above), the number of stars (objects) in the input file, and a character string
describing the contents of the catalog to be used as a title. The program will
then execute and produce an output file with the file name BINARY.CAT which can
be used by the Floppy Almanac.  You may, of course, later rename this file.

The Floppy Almanac can always be obtained by downloading the latest version 
from the NOVAC computer bulletin board system.  It is run by the Northern 
Virginia Astronomy Club and the call is always free except for pertinent long 
distance charges.  The number is 703-256-4777 and is currently running at 1200 
baud, no parity, eight data bits and one stop bit.


